{"name":"Shooting Star","hex":"#ecf0eb","fetched":"2026-10-13","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"shooting star","whole":true,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A meteor, especially a streak of light in the night sky, caused by a meteoroid burning up as it enters the Earth's atmosphere.","Any of several wild flowering plants in the genus Primula (synonym Dodecatheon), mostly found in Western North America."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/shooting_star"}],"entries":[{"title":"Shooting Star (2015 film)","blocks":[{"text":"Shooting Star is a short film co-produced between Bulgaria and Italy starring Stefka Yanorova, Stefan Popov and Kalia Kamenova written and directed by Lyubo Yonchev."}],"link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Shooting_Star_(2015_film)"},{"title":"Shooting Star (2020 film)","blocks":[{"text":"Shooting Star is a 2020 Canadian short drama film, written and directed by Ariane Louis-Seize. The film stars Marguerite Bouchard as Chloé, a teenage girl travelling with her mother Nathalie and her mother's boyfriend Christopher to observe shooting stars, only to begin developing a sexual and romantic attraction of her own to Christopher."}],"link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Shooting_Star_(2020_film)"},{"title":"Shooting Star (band)","blocks":[{"text":"Shooting Star is an American rock band from Kansas City, Missouri, formed in the late 1970s. After gaining popularity in the Kansas City area, Shooting Star became the first American group to sign with Virgin Records. They recorded their debut album in England in May and June of 1979 with producer Gus Dudgeon. The band gained national exposure when a number of songs garnered moderate airplay on album-oriented rock radio stations in the US."}],"link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Shooting_Star_(band)"},{"title":"Meteor","blocks":[{"text":"A meteor, known colloquially as a shooting star, is a glowing streak of a small body going through Earth's atmosphere, after being heated to incandescence by collisions with air molecules in the upper atmosphere, creating a streak of light via its rapid motion and sometimes also by shedding glowing material in its wake. Meteors typically occur in the mesosphere at altitudes from 76–100 kilometres. The root word meteor comes from the Greek μετεωρίτης (meteōrítēs), meaning \"high in the air\"."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/95/Cosmic_Fireball_Falling_Over_ALMA.jpg/330px-Cosmic_Fireball_Falling_Over_ALMA.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Meteor"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/4e/P10220XX.01.49.MESZ.14.08.2019.Berlin.Perseiden.x1.0.gif/500px-P10220XX.01.49.MESZ.14.08.2019.Berlin.Perseiden.x1.0.gif?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/4e/P10220XX.01.49.MESZ.14.08.2019.Berlin.Perseiden.x1.0.gif/500px-P10220XX.01.49.MESZ.14.08.2019.Berlin.Perseiden.x1.0.gif?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"A meteoroid of the Perseids with a size of about 1 cm (3⁄8 in) entering the earth's atmosphere in real time. The meteoroid is at the bright head of the trail, and the ionisation of the mesosphere is still visible in the tail.","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AP10220XX.01.49.MESZ.14.08.2019.Berlin.Perseiden.x1.0.gif"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/a7/Leonid_Meteor.jpg/500px-Leonid_Meteor.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/a7/Leonid_Meteor.jpg/1280px-Leonid_Meteor.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/a7/Leonid_Meteor.jpg/500px-Leonid_Meteor.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnai